                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "multitrack":</h3><p>The <b>multitrack</b> tag is used to describe microphones who have multiple audio outputs that can be tracked separately, enabling the mix engineer to alter the effective pickup pattern during mixdown.</p><p>Many of these mics employ dual back-to-back cardioid capsules, with separate output circuits for each.</p><p>Two such signals can be combined to form an infinite variety of mono signals:</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/sennheiser/MKH800-pattern-control.png"><img src="http://recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/sennheiser/MKH800-pattern-control-sm.png" class="thumb shaft" alt="Pattern control technique for MKH-800 dual-capsule microphone" width="200" /></a><br />For example, opposing cardioids mixed at equal gain and equal polarity create an omnidirectional signal. Flipping the parity on one or the other creates a figure-of-8 signal. Attenuating the polarity-reversed signal by 10dB creates a hypercardioid signal.</p><p>The above illustration depicts further examples, but note that any degree of directionality, favoring either the &#8216;front&#8217; or &#8216;back&#8217; capsule, can be attained by manipulation of relative gain and phase controls.</p>
